steps.body:
*Compile-Time C++: constexpr, static_assert, and Type Traits* split `mid` into an integer version and a floating-point version with `std::enable_if_t`, and a type that fit neither got `no type named 'type' in 'struct std::enable_if<false, int>'`, a message about the trick rather than about your type. C++20 states the requirement itself. A **concept** is a named test on a type, true or false while compiling, and `<concepts>` has the common ones: `std::integral`, `std::floating_point`, `std::same_as`, and `std::convertible_to`. Write one where `typename` was:

```cpp harness
// -std=c++20
#include <concepts>
#include <iostream>

{{code}}

int main() {
    std::cout << mid(10125, 10126) << ' ' << mid(101.25, 101.26) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <std::integral T>
T mid(T bid, T ask) {
    return bid + (ask - bid) / 2;
}

template <std::floating_point T>
T mid(T bid, T ask) {
    return (bid + ask) / 2;
}
```

A concept of your own lists, in a `requires` expression, what must compile for the type. That lesson's pricer took any `Payoff` type and trusted it to be callable. Now it checks:

```cpp harness
// -std=c++20
#include <algorithm>
#include <concepts>
#include <iostream>

struct Call {
    double operator()(double S, double K) const { return std::max(S - K, 0.0); }
};

{{code}}

int main() {
    static_assert(Payoff<Call>);
    std::cout << price(Call{}, 100.0, 95.0) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
template <typename P>
concept Payoff = requires(const P& p, double S, double K) {
    { p(S, K) } -> std::convertible_to<double>;
};

template <Payoff P>
double price(const P& p, double S, double K) {
    return 0.5 * (p(S * 1.1, K) + p(S * 0.9, K));
}
```

The line `{ p(S, K) } -> std::convertible_to<double>;` requires that the call compiles and that its result converts to a `double`. A concept is also a `bool` constant, so `static_assert(Payoff<Call>);` tests a type on its own. Pass something else, like a `Quote` struct that holds a bid and an ask, and g++ names the broken promise at your call, `use of function ... with unsatisfied constraints`, then `the required expression 'p(S, K)' is invalid`:

```cpp harness
