// -std=c++20
#include <cmath>
#include <concepts>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>

template <std::integral T>
bool is_on_grid(T price, T tick) {
    return price % tick == 0;
}

template <std::floating_point T>
bool is_on_grid(T price, T tick) {
    const T steps = price / tick;
    return std::abs(steps - std::round(steps)) < 1e-9;
}

template <typename F>
concept Fee = requires(const F& f, int qty, double px) {
    { f(qty, px) } -> std::convertible_to<double>;
};

struct Flat {
    double operator()(int, double) const { return 1.0; }
};
struct Bps {
    double bps;
    double operator()(int qty, double px) const { return qty * px * bps / 10000.0; }
};
struct Quote {
    double bid, ask;
};

static_assert(Fee<Flat> && Fee<Bps>);
static_assert(!Fee<Quote>);

template <Fee F>
double total_cost(const F& fee, int qty, double px) {
    return qty * px + fee(qty, px);
}

int main() {
    long long price = 0;
    long long tick = 0;
    double decimal_price = 0.0;
    double decimal_tick = 0.0;
    int qty = 0;
    double px = 0.0;
    std::cin >> price >> tick >> decimal_price >> decimal_tick >> qty >> px;
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << price << " on grid " << tick << ": " << is_on_grid(price, tick) << '\n';
    std::cout << decimal_price << " on grid " << decimal_tick << ": " << is_on_grid(decimal_price, decimal_tick) << '\n';
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2);
    std::cout << "flat: " << total_cost(Flat{}, qty, px) << '\n';
    std::cout << "bps: " << total_cost(Bps{4.0}, qty, px) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
Every algorithm call in this course has taken two iterators, as in `std::sort(v.begin(), v.end())`, and every filter has been a hand-written loop or a copy into a new vector. C++20's ranges library takes whole containers and chains lazy steps over them.

- The algorithms in `std::ranges`, from `<algorithm>`, take the container itself: `std::ranges::sort(v)`, `std::ranges::find(v, x)`, `std::ranges::count_if(v, pred)`. Most also take a **projection**, a member or function applied to each element before comparing, so `std::ranges::sort(fills, {}, &Fill::px)` sorts fills by price with no lambda. The `{}` asks for the default comparison, `<`.
- A **view**, from `<ranges>`, describes a sequence without owning one. `std::views::filter(pred)` keeps the elements that pass, `std::views::transform(f)` maps each one, and `std::views::take(n)`, `std::views::reverse`, and `std::views::iota(a, b)`, the integers from `a` up to `b - 1`, do what they say. `|` chains them, and nothing runs until a loop pulls elements through, so a pipeline copies and allocates nothing.

```cpp harness
// -std=c++20
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <ranges>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    {{code}}
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::vector<int> qty = {300, -50, 200, -40, 30};
auto doubled_buys = qty | std::views::filter([](int q) { return q > 0; })
                        | std::views::transform([](int q) { return q * 2; });
for (int q : doubled_buys) std::cout << q << ' ';  // 600 400 60
std::cout << '\n';
std::ranges::sort(qty);
std::cout << qty.front() << '\n';  // -50
```

C++23 adds `std::ranges::to<std::vector>()`, which collects a view into a new container. This Mac's clang has it, and g++ 10.2 doesn't.

`std::span<T>`, from `<span>`, does for arrays what `std::string_view` does for text: a pointer and a length, 16 bytes, viewing elements someone else owns. A function that takes a `std::span<const double>` accepts a `std::vector`, a `std::array`, or a plain array, with no copy and no template. C++17 needed a `const std::vector<double>&`, for vectors only, or the separate pointer and size *Pointers* passed with an array. `first(n)`, `last(n)`, and `subspan(offset, n)` cut smaller windows:

```cpp
// -std=c++20
#include <array>
#include <iostream>
#include <span>
#include <vector>

double average(std::span<const double> px) {
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (double p : px) sum += p;
    return px.empty() ? 0.0 : sum / px.size();
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<double> v = {101.25, 101.27, 101.26};
    const std::array<double, 2> a = {99.5, 100.5};
    const double c[3] = {1.0, 2.0, 4.5};
    std::cout << average(v) << ' ' << average(a) << ' ' << average(c) << '\n';  // 101.26 100 2.5
    std::cout << average(std::span(v).last(2)) << '\n';                        // 101.265
    return 0;
}
```

A span has the trap of *`string_view` and Fast Parsing*: it dangles when the vector it views grows or dies. A few smaller C++20 additions run here too: `std::string_view::starts_with`, `std::map::contains`, `std::erase_if(v, pred)`, which removes every matching element in one call, and `std::endian` from `<bit>`, which names the machine's byte order. Others need a newer library than g++ 10.2's: `<chrono>` calendars like `year_month_day`, time zones through `zoned_time`, and a leap-second-aware `utc_clock` for *Time in C++: chrono, Clocks, and Timestamps*, and `std::bit_cast`, with C++23's `std::byteswap`, for the `memcpy` punning and byte swaps of *Binary Formats: Bit-Fields, Unions, and Endianness*. Apple clang 21 has all of those but `zoned_time` and `utc_clock`.

