// -std=c++20
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <ranges>
#include <span>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Quote {
    std::string symbol;
    std::int64_t bid;
    std::int64_t ask;
};

std::int64_t widest(std::span<const Quote> quotes) {
    std::int64_t best = 0;
    for (const Quote& q : quotes) best = std::max(best, q.ask - q.bid);
    return best;
}

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::int64_t min_spread = 0;
    std::cin >> n >> min_spread;
    std::vector<Quote> quotes(n);
    for (Quote& q : quotes) std::cin >> q.symbol >> q.bid >> q.ask;

    std::ranges::sort(quotes, {}, &Quote::bid);
    for (const Quote& q : quotes) std::cout << q.symbol << ' ' << q.bid << ' ' << q.ask << '\n';

    auto wide = quotes
        | std::views::filter([min_spread](const Quote& q) { return q.ask - q.bid >= min_spread; })
        | std::views::transform(&Quote::symbol);
    std::cout << "wide:";
    for (const std::string& s : wide) std::cout << ' ' << s;
    std::cout << '\n';

    const std::span<const Quote> all(quotes);
    std::cout << "widest " << widest(all) << ", top two " << widest(all.last(2)) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
*Operator Overloading* wrote `operator==`, then `operator!=`, since C++17 doesn't make one from the other, then `operator<` with `std::tie`. C++20 gives all six comparisons for one line:

```cpp harness
// -std=c++20
#include <compare>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

{{code}}

int main() {
    const Level a{19275, 100};
    const Level b{19275, 300};
    std::cout << (a < b) << (a == b) << (a != b) << (a >= b) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
struct Level {
    std::int64_t px;
    std::int64_t qty;
    auto operator<=>(const Level&) const = default;
};
```

`<=>` is the **three-way comparison**, nicknamed the spaceship operator: one call answers less, equal, or greater. Defaulted, it compares the members in declaration order, like the `std::tie` version, and declares a defaulted `==` along with it. The compiler then rewrites `a < b` as `(a <=> b) < 0` and `a != b` as `!(a == b)`, so all six operators work, and `std::set<Level>` and `std::ranges::sort` need nothing more. The result's type says how strict the order is: `std::strong_ordering` for integer members, and `std::partial_ordering` when a member is a `double`, since a NaN is neither less than, equal to, nor greater than anything.

**Designated initializers** name the fields in braces, as in `Level c{.px = 19280};`. Fields you skip are value-initialized, as with short braces in *Initialization: Braces, Narrowing, and the Most Vexing Parse*. The names must come in declaration order, or g++ says `designator order for field 'Tick::ts_ns' does not match declaration order`, and they can't be mixed with plain values. g++ also accepts them in C++17 mode, as an extension, while clang warns `designated initializers are a C++20 extension`, so build with `-std=c++20` when you use them.

**std::format** (C++20) and **std::print** (C++23) replace `std::setw`, `std::fixed`, and `std::setprecision` with a format string checked while compiling. g++ 10.2 has neither, `fatal error: format: No such file or directory`, since `<format>` arrived in g++ 13. This is Apple clang 21 on a Mac, `clang++ -std=c++23`:

```cpp norun
#include <format>
#include <print>
#include <string>

int main() {
    const std::string line = std::format("{:<6}|{:>8.2f}|{:>5}", "AAPL", 192.75, 300);
    std::println("[{}] {} chars", line, line.size());
    std::println("{:08.3f} {:+d} {:#x} {:.2e}", 3.14159, 42, 255, 0.000123);
    std::println("{1} before {0}", "second", "first");
    return 0;
}
```

```text
[AAPL  |  192.75|  300] 21 chars
0003.142 +42 0xff 1.23e-04
first before second
```

`{:<6}` pads to 6 and aligns left, `{:>8.2f}` right-aligns 2 decimals in 8, and `{1}` picks an argument by position. A value that doesn't fit its spec is a compile error: `std::format("{:d}", 192.75)` fails with `call to consteval function 'std::basic_format_string<char, double>::basic_format_string<char[5]>' is not a constant expression`, because the format string's constructor is `consteval`.

