steps.body:
A generator hands out values one at a time, on demand: the fields of a message, the levels of a price grid, the steps of a simulated path. In C++17 that's a class that keeps its loop's state in members between calls. A **coroutine** is a function that can pause and resume instead. `co_yield value;` hands a value to the caller and pauses with every local variable kept, the next resume carries on right after it, and `co_return;` ends it. Using either keyword is what makes a function a coroutine:

```cpp harness
// -std=c++20 -fcoroutines
#include <coroutine>
#include <exception>
#include <iostream>
#include <string_view>
#include <utility>

template <typename T>
class Generator {
public:
    struct promise_type {  // the compiler looks for this name
        T current{};
        Generator get_return_object() {
            return Generator{std::coroutine_handle<promise_type>::from_promise(*this)};
        }
        std::suspend_always initial_suspend() noexcept { return {}; }  // pause before the body
        std::suspend_always final_suspend() noexcept { return {}; }    // and at its end
        std::suspend_always yield_value(T value) noexcept {            // co_yield: keep it, pause
            current = value;
            return {};
        }
        void return_void() noexcept {}
        void unhandled_exception() { std::terminate(); }
    };

    explicit Generator(std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> h) : h_(h) {}
    Generator(Generator&& other) noexcept : h_(std::exchange(other.h_, {})) {}  // take it, leave other empty
    ~Generator() {
        if (h_) h_.destroy();
    }
    bool next() {  // run to the next co_yield, false once the body has ended
        h_.resume();
        return !h_.done();
    }
    T value() const { return h_.promise().current; }

private:
    std::coroutine_handle<promise_type> h_;
};

{{code}}

int main() {
    Generator<std::string_view> g = fields("35=D|55=AAPL|44=19275", '|');
    while (g.next()) {
        std::cout << g.value() << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
Generator<std::string_view> fields(std::string_view msg, char sep) {
    while (true) {
        const std::size_t bar = msg.find(sep);
        co_yield msg.substr(0, bar);
        if (bar == std::string_view::npos) co_return;
        msg.remove_prefix(bar + 1);
    }
}
```

C++20 has the mechanism but no generator type, so the example writes a small `Generator<T>`. The compiler requires its `promise_type`, which stores the yielded value and says where to pause, and a `std::coroutine_handle` resumes the coroutine and destroys it. C++23's `std::generator` is that class in the library, ready for a range-for loop, but neither g++ 10.2 nor this Mac's libc++ has it yet. g++ 10 also wants `-fcoroutines` on the first line. `co_yield` is one use of the machinery: *Coroutines in Depth: Awaitables, Tasks, and an Event Loop*, the next lesson, opens up the promise and builds the awaiters and the `Task` that let a coroutine wait for an event.

### Past g++ 10.2

`std::expected<T, E>` (C++23, in g++ 12 and later) is the `Result` of *Errors in Fast Code: noexcept, Error Codes, and Results* in the library: `return std::unexpected(reason);` for a failure, and `if (r)` or `has_value()`, `*r`, `error()`, `value_or()`, and `transform` to read one. That lesson's price parser with `Result` swapped for it, on a Mac with `clang++ -std=c++23`:

```cpp norun
std::expected<std::int64_t, ParseError> parse_price(std::string_view text) {
    if (text.size() > 9) return std::unexpected(ParseError::TooLong);
    // ... the same checks, each failure a std::unexpected(...)
    return ticks;
}

for (std::string_view token : {"19275", "19x75", "0"}) {
    const auto r = parse_price(token);
    if (r) {
        std::println("{} -> {}", token, *r);
    } else {
        std::println("{} -> error {}", token, static_cast<int>(r.error()));
    }
}
const auto dollars = parse_price("41010").transform([](std::int64_t t) { return t / 100.0; });
std::println("{:.2f} {} {}", *dollars, parse_price("abc").value_or(0),
             sizeof(std::expected<std::int64_t, ParseError>));
```

```text
19275 -> 19275
19x75 -> error 0
0 -> error 2
410.10 0 16
```

**Modules** (C++20) replace the headers and `#include`s of *Headers, Translation Units, and the Linker*. A module says what it exports, the compiler builds it once into a binary file, and `import prices;` reads that file instead of pasting text. On this Mac `#include <iostream>` alone expands to about 84,000 lines under `-std=c++20` (64,192 under C++17, as *From Source to Executable: Preprocess, Compile, Link* counted), compiled again in every file that includes it. A name a module doesn't export stays invisible outside it. g++ 10.2 has no modules (`'import' does not name a type`), and Apple clang 21 turns them on with `-fcxx-modules`:

```cpp norun
// prices.cppm
export module prices;

export long long to_ticks(long long dollars, long long cents) {
    return dollars * 100 + cents;
}

long long whole_lots(long long qty) { return qty / 100; }  // not exported
// main.cpp
#include <iostream>
import prices;

int main() {
    std::cout << to_ticks(192, 75) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```text
$ clang++ -std=c++20 -fcxx-modules --precompile prices.cppm -o prices.pcm
$ clang++ -std=c++20 -fcxx-modules -fmodule-file=prices=prices.pcm main.cpp prices.pcm -o main
$ ./main
19275
```

A file that calls `whole_lots` gets `error: use of undeclared identifier 'whole_lots'`. C++20 also adds `std::jthread`, a thread that joins itself when it's destroyed and can be asked to stop through a `std::stop_token`, and g++ 10.2 does have that one, along with the `[[likely]]` and `[[unlikely]]` hints that lay out a branch for its usual path. *Threads, Data Races, and Mutexes*, which opens the Concurrency section later in the track, starts from `std::thread`. Past g++ 10.2 there's more: `std::source_location`, a log line's file and line without a macro, `std::latch`, `std::barrier`, and `std::counting_semaphore` for threads that wait on each other, and in C++23 `std::flat_map`, a sorted map kept in vectors, and `std::mdspan`, a view of a flat buffer as a matrix. *Beyond One Producer: MPMC Queues, ABA, and Seqlocks*, in the Concurrency section, runs the three waiting tools on a Mac.

